#TOP description "The registered payload schema DOCUMENT for fact2 relation payloads, named by identity-and-evidence section 3 and native-evidence section 4.4. fact.payloadSchemaDigest is the raw SHA-256 of the EXACT FULL BYTES of this file; validation uses the selector the payload registry row names, exactly as the native and workflow import registries already work. This document has no root type on purpose: it is a multi-record bundle, and a bundle is admitted through its registry row, never by validating a payload against the whole document. ENCODING SUCCESSOR: fact2 payloads are canonical JSON C, not the inherited deterministic-CBOR profile. Every logical restriction that profile carried survives here as an admission rule - NFC text, no negative integers, closed field sets, enums, per-rung required/forbidden fields and the universe rule. Historical FactRecord1 CBOR bytes and their provenance are unchanged and remain valid history; they are never a fact2 preimage."
#TOP x-opensip-relation-registry {"standing":"Normative. Closed at 13 relations: the twelve inherited from fact-plane.v1 relationPayloadSchemaRegistryV1 plus native-evidence section 4.4 unresolved-edge.","document":"foundation/relation-payload-schemas.v2.json","payloadSchemaDigest":"raw SHA-256 of the exact full bytes of that file","codec":"C (identity-and-evidence section 3 canonical JSON)","inheritedFrom":"docs/coop/artifacts/fact-plane.v1.json $.factRecordContractV1.relationPayloadSchemaRegistryV1","ladderAuthority":"This registry is the SINGLE authority for every relation's resolution ladder, as identity-and-evidence section 3 states. `ladder` is that relation's total order over its OWN resolution methods, listed weakest-first, inherited verbatim from fact-plane.v1 relationRegistry.relations[].ladder (plus [observed] for unresolved-edge). Every other copy in the kit - native_evidence_model.v2.py LADDERS and capability-manifest-domains.v2.json RELATION-LADDER-DOMAIN-V2.ladders - is a declared MIRROR of this array and is drift-checked against it exactly and IN ORDER. A one-rung ladder is not a deficiency; it means the relation is fully determined at that rung.","rungsAreFieldRulesNotTheLadder":"`rungs` is a different table: the per-rung required/forbidden PAYLOAD FIELD rules. It is legitimately the empty object for a relation with no per-rung field variance, which is every single-rung relation. Reading `rungs` as the ladder is the defect this key exists to prevent: an empty field-rule table must never be read as an absent ladder, and never as a licence to skip ladder membership. Membership is checked against `ladder`, always, with no fallback.","membershipRule":"A resolution is admissible for a relation only if it is a member of THAT relation's `ladder`. A rung that is a member of another relation's ladder is refused, not accepted as a live vocabulary token; the flat rung vocabulary published elsewhere is necessary but never sufficient. Order comparison is the index within one relation's ladder; nothing orders a rung of one relation against a rung of another, and there is no global rank.","subjectKindLaw":"What a subject-scope's `subjects` entries IDENTIFY, per relation. `source-path`: each subject is a path in the snapshot this scope names, so the scope's own extent is exactly those paths - `file` and `vcs-change` bind it through their registry snapshotJoins pathField, and `clones` binds it through bodyIdentityJoin, whose single anchor IS the body span the identity is over. `package-name`: a package identifier, not a path. `symbol`: an opaque SubjectIdV1 that the record associates with no path; the enumerator's attribution of symbols to files is trusted and is NOT re-derivable from the retained Run, so no per-subject path law is possible for these relations and any capability question about them can only be answered at the coarser retained extent. That limit is stated rather than worked around: inventing symbol-to-path parsing would be fabricated evidence.","anchorLaw":{"stan
#TOP x-opensip-digest-law {"standing":"The closing digest law of identity-and-evidence section 3, extended to this bundle. Every DigestHex, Sha256Text and CanonicalPath field here carries x-opensip-digest naming its representation, producing preimage, codec, retention and owning authority. There is no default and no residue: a new such field added without an annotation is inadmissible. Coverage is asserted by consumed CHECKS in the Run closure, not by counting annotations.","representations":["raw-artifact","snapshot-path","framed-body-identity"],"retention":{"preimage":"the exact preimage bytes are retained under this digest and the closure re-hashes them","snapshot-inventoried":"the value names a path in the analysed snapshot inventory, joined there","provider-output-retained":"the canonical payload is a versioned normalizer output the host cannot recompute; its exact framed preimage is retained so an independent replay can re-hash it","not-joined":"the value names a state outside the analysed snapshot and is deliberately joined to nothing; the reason is stated on the field and the closure records the exemption rather than leaving it as residue"},"enforcement":"identity-model close_run applies the per-relation snapshotJoins and bodyIdentity joins below to EVERY owning fact, after registered-selector validation and independently of the payload decode cache. A payload alone cannot decide snapshot truth, so the enclosing fact, the snapshot, the retained blobs and the bound native context are required owner context.","residueRule":"Consumed, not declared. Every x-opensip-digest field of a relation selector must be reachable from that relation registry entry snapshotJoins or bodyIdentityJoin, or must declare retention not-joined with its reason. An annotated field with no join, a join naming a field the selector does not have, and an unannotated digest/path field are each inadmissible."}
